-1.1 **The projectors exist and are polynomials in the $x_r$.** Since the $x_r$ commute pairwise, the subalgebra they generate is the image of $\mathbb C[t_1,\dots,t_d]$, and the relation $(x_r-m_1)\cdots(x_r-m_l)=0$ holds for every $r$. Write $\{a_1<\cdots<a_s\}$ for the distinct values among $m_1,\dots,m_l$ with multiplicities $\mu_u\ge1$, so that $\prod_{u=1}^{s}(t-a_u)^{\mu_u}=\prod_{j=1}^{l}(t-m_j)$. The ideals $(t-a_u)^{\mu_u}$ of $\mathbb C[t]$ are pairwise comaximal, so [F5] applied to $R=\mathbb C[t]$ gives polynomials $E_u(t)\in\mathbb C[t]$ with $E_u\equiv1\bmod (t-a_u)^{\mu_u}$ and $E_u\equiv0\bmod (t-a_v)^{\mu_v}$ for $v\ne u$. Extend to all integers by $E_j:=E_u$ for $j=a_u$ and $E_j:=0$ for $j\notin\{a_1,\dots,a_s\}$. Then the $E_j(x_r)$ are mutually orthogonal idempotents of the commutative algebra $\mathbb C[x_1,\dots,x_d]$ with $\sum_{j\in\mathbb Z}E_j(x_r)=1$, and comparison with the defining relation shows that $E_u(x_r)$ projects onto the generalized eigenspace of $x_r$ at $a_u$. Putting $e(i):=\prod_{r=1}^{d}E_{i_r}(x_r)$ for $i=(i_1,\dots,i_d)\in\mathbb Z^d$ gives mutually orthogonal idempotents in $\mathbb C[x_1,\dots,x_d]$ with $\sum_i e(i)=1$; only finitely many are nonzero because each $x_r$ has at most $s$ generalized eigenvalues. For a finite-dimensional $H_d(m)$-module $M$ the commuting endomorphisms $x_r$ are simultaneously triangularizable on each generalized joint eigenspace, so $e(i)M=\{v\in M:(x_r-i_r)^Nv=0\ \text{for all }r\text{ and }N\gg0\}$; this is the generalized weight space decomposition of $M$. [F5, given, construct, algebra]
