-1.2 **The stable realization and the centre comparison.** Assume first that the data lie in an admissible stable realization; then $H_d(m)\cong\operatorname{End}_{\mathcal O}(T_\alpha)^{op}$ for the prinjective detector $T_\alpha$ of the genuine parabolic block $\mathcal O_\alpha$ of [[def-integral-parabolic-category-o-block-for-higher-level-schur-weyl-duality]], by [[thm-higher-level-schur-weyl-endomorphism-isomorphism]]. Choose a finite projective generator $Q$ of $\mathcal O_\alpha$ with $T_\alpha$ a direct summand, and put $A:=\operatorname{End}_{\mathcal O}(Q)^{op}$ and $H:=\operatorname{End}_{\mathcal O}(T_\alpha)^{op}=eAe$, where $e$ is the idempotent of the summand $T_\alpha\subseteq Q$. Only finitely many isomorphism classes of indecomposable projectives occur, so this is a finite selection. Full faithfulness of the Schur quotient on all projectives ([[thm-the-whittaker-functor-is-exact-and-fully-faithful-on-the-required-projectives]]) gives an algebra isomorphism $A\cong\operatorname{End}_H(\pi Q)^{op}$, where $\pi$ is the quotient functor onto the modules over $H$. Since $Q$ contains a representative of every projective, $\pi Q$ contains the image of every projective of $\mathcal O_\alpha$; as a projective generator of $H\text{-}\mathrm{mod}$, the module $\pi Q$ is a progenerator, and the centre of the endomorphism ring of a progenerator is Morita invariant: $Z(A)\cong Z(H)$. Concretely, a central $h\in H$ acts on $\pi Q$ by left multiplication, which is a central $H$-linear endomorphism and therefore, by full faithfulness on the projective $Q$, is the image of a unique central element of $A$; restricting a central element of $A$ to $eAe=H$ inverts this correspondence, the composite $Z(H)\to Z(A)\to Z(H)$ being the identity because it is the identity on $\pi T_\alpha=H$, and the other composite being the identity because for every indecomposable summand $Q'\subseteq Q$ the module $\pi Q'$ is generated by its images under $H$-linear maps from the summands of $\pi T_\alpha$ (a projective cover is generated by any of its simple quotients and $\pi Q'$ either is such a cover or is a non-projective image whose generators are reached from the covers by quotient maps), so agreement on $\pi T_\alpha$ forces agreement on $\pi Q'$ and hence on $\pi Q$. [given, construct, algebra]
